    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>The sources give casualties of 165,000 (<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jordanes">Jordanes</a>) and almost 300,000 (<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hydatius">Hydatius</a>), figures that are implausibly high, though all the primary sources record the battle as exceptionally bloody.</li><li>Many historians (Bury, Hughes, Bachrach, Kim) hold that the siege of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/orleans">Aurelianum</a>, not the battle, was the turning point of the campaign, since the battle was fought as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/attila">Attila</a> was already leaving <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaul">Gaul</a>.</li><li>Contemporary sources describe the battle as inconclusive and credit <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/flavius-aetius">Aetius</a>; later writers made it a Gothic victory and a point of Gothic pride.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>The exact site of the battle is still unknown: scholars place it near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chalons-en-champagne">Châlons-en-Champagne</a> or <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/troyes">Troyes</a>, and a rich burial found at Pouan-les-Vallées in 1842, first thought to be <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theodoric-i">Theodoric</a>'s, has not been shown to be related to the battle.</p></aside>
    </section>

