    <section class="rise-fall" id="timeline" aria-labelledby="timeline-title">
      <h2 class="profile-section-title" id="timeline-title">Timeline</h2>
      <ol class="rf-events"><li><span class="rf-year">449</span><span class="rf-text">The King of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frankish-kingdom">Franks</a> dies and his two sons dispute the succession, the elder seeking <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/attila">Attila</a>'s help and the younger siding with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/flavius-aetius">Aetius</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">450</span><span class="rf-text">Honoria sends a ring and a request for help to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/attila">Attila</a>, who claims half the empire as a dowry</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">451</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/attila">Attila</a> crosses the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhine">Rhine</a> and sacks <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/metz">Divodurum</a> (Metz) on 7 April</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">451</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/attila">Attila</a>'s final assault on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/orleans">Aurelianum</a> on 14 June is broken off as the Roman coalition approaches</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">c. 451</span><span class="rf-text">The two armies meet on the Catalaunian Plains, about 20 June, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theodoric-i">Theodoric</a> is killed</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">451</span><span class="rf-text">Thorismund returns to Tolosa and becomes king of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/visigoths">Visigoths</a>, while <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/attila">Attila</a> leaves <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaul">Gaul</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">452</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/attila">Attila</a> invades <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>, razes <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aquileia">Aquileia</a> and ravages the Po Valley before withdrawing</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">453</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/attila">Attila</a> dies</span></li></ol>
    </section>

    <section class="battle-field" id="how-it-was-fought" data-battle="battle-of-the-catalaunian-plains" data-v="025f0c46a2" aria-labelledby="battle-field-title">
      <h2 class="profile-section-title" id="battle-field-title">How it was fought</h2>
      <p class="bf-lede">The field and the armies, phase by phase. Press Play, or step through with Next and Back.</p>
      <ol class="bf-phases"><li><b>The two armies deploy along the ridge</b> Both sides spent the day deploying their coalition armies. Attila's Huns stood in the centre with their vassals on the wings, while the Romans took the left, the Alans the centre and the Visigoths the right.</li><li><b>The Romans take the left and repulse the Gepids</b> Attila's forces reached the right side of the ridge first. The Romans then arrived on the left side and drove back the Gepids as they came up.</li><li><b>The Alans and Thorismund secure the centre</b> The Alans and the Visigoths under Thorismund fought their way up the ridge and took its centre, which they held against Attila.</li><li><b>The Visigoths try to take the right of the ridge</b> Theodoric led his Visigoths against the Amali Goths on the right. He was killed in the assault, and the rest of his army did not know of his death.</li><li><b>Thorismund drives back the Hunnic centre</b> Thorismund found Attila's place in the Hunnic line and attacked the Hunnic centre, nearly killing Attila. The Hunnic centre was forced to retreat.</li><li><b>Attila withdraws into his camp as night falls</b> Attila sought refuge in his wagon camp. When night fell, Thorismund, returning towards friendly lines, entered the camp by mistake and was wounded in the fighting there.</li></ol>
    </section>


    <div class="profile-section-block" id="background">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Background</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/valentinian-iii"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/valentinian-iii.jpg" alt="Valentinian III" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/valentinian-iii">Valentinian III</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bust_of_Valentinian_III,_Louvre_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Tony Querrec (photo), CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>By 450 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> had restored their authority in much of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaul">Gaul</a>, although control over the provinces beyond <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> was continuing to diminish. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armorica">Armorica</a> was only nominally imperial, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germanic-peoples">Germanic peoples</a> on Roman land were bound by treaty as foederati under their own leaders. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/visigoths">Visigoths</a> on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/garonne">Garonne</a> were restive but held to their treaty, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burgundians">Burgundians</a> awaited an opening for revolt, and the Alans on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/loire">Loire</a>, loyal since 411, were more dependable.</p><p>The sources give different reasons for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/attila">Attila</a>'s invasion. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jordanes">Jordanes</a> states that the Vandal king Genseric enticed him to attack the Visigoths, but Jordanes's account of Gothic history is notoriously unreliable, and modern scholars think the explanation projects later events onto Attila's time. Other contemporary writers point to Justa Grata Honoria, sister of the emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/valentinian-iii">Valentinian III</a>, who in 450 sent the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eunuch">eunuch</a> Hyacinthus to Attila asking for help in escaping her confinement. Allegedly Attila took this as an offer of marriage and claimed half the empire as a dowry; Valentinian refused. Ian Hughes suggests she was using Attila's status as honorary magister militum as political leverage. A Frankish succession dispute added to it: the elder son of the dead king sought Attila's help, and the younger sided with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/flavius-aetius">Aetius</a>, who adopted him.</p><p>Attila crossed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhine">Rhine</a> early in 451 and sacked <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/metz">Metz</a> on 7 April, which Schultheis thinks may be a literary trope. By June he had reached <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/orleans">Aurelianum</a> (Orléans), whose people shut their gates, and he besieged it. His final assault on 14 June was broken off because the Roman coalition was approaching.</p></div><details class="sec-src"><summary>Sources for this section</summary><p class="sec-src-n">Works that Wikipedia&#8217;s article &#8220;Battle of the Catalaunian Plains&#8221; cites where it covers Prelude:</p><ul><li>Ian Hughes, Aetius: Attila's Nemesis (Pen and Sword Books Ltd., 2012)</li><li>Bernard Bachrach, A History of the Alans in the West (University of Minnesota Press, 1973)</li><li>Evan Schultheis, The Battle of the Catalaunian Fields, AD 451: Flavius Aetius, Attila the Hun, and the Transformation of Gaul (Pen &amp; Sword Books Ltd., 2019)</li></ul></details>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="forces">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">The Two Sides</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/notitia-dignitatum"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/notitia-dignitatum.jpg" alt="Notitia Dignitatum" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/notitia-dignitatum">Notitia Dignitatum</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bodl_Canon.Misc.378_roll159B_frame28.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Unknown author, CC BY 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Both armies were made up of many peoples. Besides the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a>, Alans and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/visigoths">Visigoths</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jordanes">Jordanes</a> lists <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/flavius-aetius">Aetius</a>'s allies as the Francii, Sarmatae, Armoriciani, Liticiani, Burgundiones, Saxones, Riparii and Olibrones, together with other Celtic and German tribes. Peter Heather suggests that the Visigoths may have fielded about 25,000 men. Schultheis argues that the Germanic federates could on paper number more than 70,000 but probably numbered under 50,000, and that the Roman field army at the time of the battle was approximately 20,500 comitatenses and 18,000 limitanei. A. H. M. Jones's study of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/notitia-dignitatum">Notitia Dignitatum</a> gives just under 46,000 for units in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaul">Gaul</a> in 395 to 425, an estimate that changes with new research.</p><p>Jordanes lists the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gepids">Gepids</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ardaric">Ardaric</a> and Gothic groups led by the Amali brothers Valamir, Theodemir and Videmir among <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/attila">Attila</a>'s allies. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sidonius-apollinaris">Sidonius Apollinaris</a> gives a longer list, but E. A. Thompson suspects that some names come from literary tradition, since several peoples had disappeared centuries earlier. Thompson doubted that Attila could have fed even 30,000, and Lindner argues that the Great Hungarian Plain could support only 15,000 mounted nomads. Schultheis finds that the sources add up to more than 100,000 on paper but probably about 70,000.</p><p>On Jordanes's account the Visigoths held the right, the Romans the left, and Sangiban's Alans, of uncertain loyalty, the middle. Hughes argues that the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/huns">Huns</a> stood in the centre with their <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassals</a> on the wings, and that Aetius put the Alans in the middle because they were skilled cavalry who could fight beside Roman troops.</p></div><details class="sec-src"><summary>Sources for this section</summary><p class="sec-src-n">Works that Wikipedia&#8217;s article &#8220;Battle of the Catalaunian Plains&#8221; cites where it covers Forces, Opposing view: battle was not of macro-historical importance:</p><ul><li>Evan Schultheis, The Battle of the Catalaunian Fields, AD 451: Flavius Aetius, Attila the Hun, and the Transformation of Gaul (Pen &amp; Sword Books Ltd., 2019)</li><li>Hyun Jin Kim, The Huns, Rome, and the Birth of Europe (Cambridge University Press, 2013)</li><li>Peter Heather, The Goths (Wiley-Blackwell, 1996)</li><li>Ian Hughes, Aetius: Attila's Nemesis (Pen and Sword Books Ltd., 2012)</li></ul></details>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="course">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">How It Went</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/avitus"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/avitus.jpg" alt="Avitus" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/avitus">Avitus</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Solidus_Avitus_Arles_(obverse).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Numismatica Ars Classica NAC AG, CC BY-SA 3.0 ch</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>On learning of the invasion, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/flavius-aetius">Aetius</a> moved his army from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaul">Gaul</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sidonius-apollinaris">Sidonius Apollinaris</a> says he led few and scattered auxiliaries, which probably reflects that most of his army was already in Gaul and Sidonius's wish to favour <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/avitus">Avitus</a>. Aetius first tried to bring in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theodoric-i">Theodoric</a>, who allegedly preferred to wait and fight the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/huns">Huns</a> on his own land, and then turned to Avitus, who according to tradition persuaded Theodoric and other wavering peoples. The coalition assembled at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arles">Arles</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toulouse">Toulouse</a> and reached <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/orleans">Aurelianum</a> on 14 June. It then pursued <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/attila">Attila</a>, who was leaving Gaul. The night before the main battle, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jordanes">Jordanes</a> reports, allied <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frankish-kingdom">Franks</a> skirmished with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gepids">Gepids</a> loyal to Attila, with 15,000 dead on each side, a figure that cannot be verified.</p><p>By tradition Attila's diviners foretold disaster for the Huns and the death of one enemy leader, and he delayed until the ninth hour, about 2:30 pm. Hughes thinks the divination may be a fabrication and that both sides simply spent the day deploying. According to Jordanes, the Huns seized the right of a ridge and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> the left, and the Romans under Aetius and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/goths">Goths</a> under Thorismund won the crest. Theodoric was killed in the assault without his men noticing, thrown from his horse and trampled or slain by the spear of the Amali Andag. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/visigoths">Visigoths</a> fell on Attila's household unit and he took refuge in his wagon-fortified camp. Thorismund, returning in the dark, wandered into the camp and was wounded, and Aetius spent the night with the Goths.</p></div><details class="sec-src"><summary>Sources for this section</summary><p class="sec-src-n">Works that Wikipedia&#8217;s article &#8220;Battle of the Catalaunian Plains&#8221; cites where it covers Battle, Aftermath and reputation of the battle, Traditional view: battle was of macro-historical importance, Opposing view: battle was not of macro-historical importance:</p><ul><li>Ian Hughes, Aetius: Attila's Nemesis (Pen and Sword Books Ltd., 2012)</li><li>Hyun Jin Kim, The Huns, Rome, and the Birth of Europe (Cambridge University Press, 2013)</li><li>Meghan McEvoy, Child Emperor Rule in the Late Roman West AD 367–455 (Oxford University Press, 2013)</li><li>Simon MacDowall, Catalaunian Fields AD 451, Rome's Last Great Battle (Osprey Publishing Ltd, 2015)</li></ul></details>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="aftermath">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Aftermath</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/gregory-of-tours"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/gregory-of-tours.jpg" alt="Gregory of Tours" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/gregory-of-tours">Gregory of Tours</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Gregory_of_Tours_cour_Napoleon_Louvre.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">CC BY 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The next day the field was piled with bodies and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/huns">Huns</a> did not come out. The allies began to besiege <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/attila">Attila</a>'s camp, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jordanes">Jordanes</a> says Attila built a funeral pyre of saddles to throw himself on if it was attacked. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/visigoths">Visigoths</a> found <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theodoric-i">Theodoric</a>'s body where the dead lay thickest and bore him away with songs in sight of the enemy. Thorismund wanted to storm the camp, but <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/flavius-aetius">Aetius</a> dissuaded him, fearing, says Jordanes, that if the Huns were wholly destroyed the Visigoths would leave their allegiance to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>. Thorismund returned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toulouse">Toulouse</a> and became king without resistance. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gregory-of-tours">Gregory of Tours</a> claims that Aetius dismissed his Frankish allies in the same way and kept the booty. Hughes thinks it likely that Aetius did advise them to go, as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frankish-kingdom">Franks</a> were fighting a civil war and Thorismund had five brothers.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hydatius">Hydatius</a> says almost 300,000 men are said to have fallen, and Jordanes gives 165,000 dead, not counting the earlier skirmish. These figures are implausibly high. Attila returned in 452 and invaded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>. After a siege of three months, which Aetius had hoped would use up the campaigning season, he razed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aquileia">Aquileia</a> and ravaged the Po Valley. Aetius had no help from the federates in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaul">Gaul</a> and sent <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-leo-i">Pope Leo I</a>, Trygetius and Gennadius <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/avienus">Avienus</a> to negotiate. Attila withdrew, most likely because of famine and disease in his army, and died in 453.</p></div><details class="sec-src"><summary>Sources for this section</summary><p class="sec-src-n">Works that Wikipedia&#8217;s article &#8220;Battle of the Catalaunian Plains&#8221; cites where it covers Outcome, As a Roman victory, As a Roman defeat or indecisive, Aftermath and reputation of the battle:</p><ul><li>Ian Hughes, Aetius: Attila's Nemesis (Pen and Sword Books Ltd., 2012)</li><li>Alexander Murray, From Roman to Merovingian Gaul (University of Toronto Press, 2008)</li><li>Simon MacDowall, Catalaunian Fields AD 451, Rome's Last Great Battle (Osprey Publishing Ltd, 2015)</li><li>Evan Schultheis, The Battle of the Catalaunian Fields, AD 451: Flavius Aetius, Attila the Hun, and the Transformation of Gaul (Pen &amp; Sword Books Ltd., 2019)</li></ul></details>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="sources">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">How We Know</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/cassiodorus"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/cassiodorus.jpg" alt="Cassiodorus" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/cassiodorus">Cassiodorus</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Gesta_Theodorici_-_Flavius_Magnus_Aurelius_Cassiodorus_(c_485_-_c_580).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Fulda workshop, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The primary sources say little about the outcome apart from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jordanes">Jordanes</a>, and all stress the casualties. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hydatius">Hydatius</a>, who lived at the time, places the battle between the appearance and disappearance of Halley's Comet and says the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/huns">Huns</a> were defeated with the aid of God. Prosper, a contemporary, says the slaughter was incalculable and that the Huns appear to have been defeated because the survivors lost their taste for fighting. The Gallic Chronicle of 452 says that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/attila">Attila</a> inflicted and suffered defeat and then withdrew, and the chronicle of 511 names Tricasses, the Mauriac plain, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theodoric-i">Theodoric</a>'s death by an uncertain hand. From the early sixth century, beginning with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cassiodorus">Cassiodorus</a>, the battle came to be seen as an allied victory.</p><p>The account of Jordanes, who served as notary to Andag's son Gunthigis, is the fullest and is distorted. Hyun Jin Kim argues that it copies the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-marathon">Battle of Marathon</a>, and Franz Altheim saw a fabrication modelled on Salamis. Kim's view that Attila won has received a mixed reception and is treated with scepticism. Schultheis argues that Jordanes reworked a narrative first written by the Goth Ablabius in 471 and expanded by Cassiodorus, and that heavy losses in the retreats led to an indecisive outcome that was later embellished into a Gothic victory.</p><p>The site is unresolved. The consensus is only that it lay near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chalons-en-champagne">Châlons-en-Champagne</a> or <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/troyes">Troyes</a>. Hodgkin proposed Méry-sur-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seine">Seine</a>, Richardot La Cheppe and, in 2015, MacDowall the ridge at Montgueux, drawing on the Additamenta to Prosper, which place the battle five Roman miles from Troyes.</p></div><details class="sec-src"><summary>Sources for this section</summary><p class="sec-src-n">Works that Wikipedia&#8217;s article &#8220;Battle of the Catalaunian Plains&#8221; cites where it covers Site of the Catalaunian Plains:</p><ul><li>Simon MacDowall, Catalaunian Fields AD 451, Rome's Last Great Battle (Osprey Publishing Ltd, 2015)</li><li>Evan Schultheis, The Battle of the Catalaunian Fields, AD 451: Flavius Aetius, Attila the Hun, and the Transformation of Gaul (Pen &amp; Sword Books Ltd., 2019)</li></ul></details>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="legacy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Legacy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/edward-gibbon"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/edward-gibbon.jpg" alt="Edward Gibbon" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/edward-gibbon">Edward Gibbon</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Edward_Gibbon_by_Henry_Walton_cleaned.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Henry Walton, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Opinion on the battle's importance divides. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edward-gibbon">Edward Gibbon</a> called it the last victory achieved in the name of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/western-roman-empire">Western Roman Empire</a>. Edward Creasy called it a triumph of Christian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> over pagan savages of Asia, and John Julius Norwich wrote that the fate of western civilisation hung in the balance in 451 and 452. Modern authors have mostly moved away from this view. Geoffrey Parker called it a triumph of Roman defensive strategy, and Arther Ferrill notes that no single nation dominated either side and that two alliances fought in surprising coordination for the time. Meghan McEvoy sees <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/flavius-aetius">Aetius</a>'s use of the federates as evidence of his diplomatic and administrative skills.</p><p>J. B. Bury held that its significance has been enormously exaggerated, that the campaign had been decided by cutting <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/attila">Attila</a> off from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/orleans">Orléans</a>, and that its value lay in damaging his prestige and weakening his forces. Hughes, Bachrach and Kim agree that the defence of Orléans was the real turning point. Bury and many others think the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-nedao">Battle of Nedao</a> in 454 was more consequential for Europe, since the Hunnic hegemony disintegrated after it.</p><p>For <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaul">Gaul</a>, Hughes argues that the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/goths">Goths</a> came to believe they had not been adequately rewarded and later destroyed the Alans and besieged Orléans. Kim argues that the battle weakened the Alans, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/visigoths">Visigoths</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> and allowed Frankish and Burgundian hegemony in northern Gaul. Tackholm notes that later sources made the battle a point of Gothic pride, while Roman sources such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/procopius">Procopius</a> keep Aetius as the central figure.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>On about 20 June 451 a coalition of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/visigoths">Visigoths</a> and Alans under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/flavius-aetius">Aetius</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theodoric-i">Theodoric I</a> fought <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/attila">Attila</a>'s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/huns">Huns</a> and their <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassals</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaul">Gaul</a>. Theodoric was killed and Attila left Gaul, but the site, the casualties and the battle's importance are all disputed.</em></p>

